# From "NVC I" (Wave IV - Adventure, CD2-4) -- the first of three nonverbal
# communication exercises (continues in nvc-ii.gws and compoint.gws). The
# tape's on/off word drill runs to about 25 items with heavy ASR noise
# between them; condensed to a representative selection of the same words,
# in the same order they appear.

@segment  nvc-i
@title    NVC I — Nonverbal Communication
@levels   F12
@duration ~4m

say Here's your first exercise in NVC — nonverbal communication. Sending and receiving whole information without words: pictures, form, movement, color, energy, vibration, emotion.
pause 8
say To see why it matters: think of the word "glasses."
pause 6
say Now let the thought go.
pause 4
say Your picture might have been eyeglasses, or drinking glasses, or window glass. Words need other words to fill in what they mean. If the information had come as NVC instead, you'd have known instantly and exactly what was meant — no filling in required. That's what you're building toward.
pause 8
say Start with something simple. Look with your closed eyes at the palm of your right hand.
pause 5
say Turn it over in your mind's view. Look at the back of your hand.
pause 5
say Let it go. Just hear my voice.
pause 5
say Now the palm of your left hand.
pause 5
say Turn it over in your mind's view. The back of your hand.
pause 5
say Let it go.
pause 4
say Now, the same way — picture each of these the moment you hear it, and release it the moment I say the next one.
pause 6
say Your face.
pause 3
say A clock.
pause 3
say A triangle.
pause 3
say A circle.
pause 3
say A cube.
pause 3
say A window.
pause 3
say A house.
pause 3
say Fire.
pause 3
say Ocean waves.
pause 3
say Rain.
pause 3
say Relax.
pause 5
say Move now to Focus 12, the state of expanded awareness. Focus 12.
pause 5
say Reach out with your awareness, and perceive whatever nonverbal information is available — not words, but the wider kind. Pictures, movement, emotion, feelings you wouldn't otherwise notice.
pause 8
hold 60
say You'll remember what you perceived in detail, and translating words into this fuller sense will come easier from now on. Take that with you, back to Focus 10.
pause 6
